## A second reader for the tests {#sec:peg-oracle}

The reader above is hand-written: it walks each line with an index loop. A hand
loop can hold a mistake that the loop and its own tests share. So the tests use a
second reader that is built a different way, and check that the two agree with real
`markup`. Two readers that agree with the oracle is the proof, the same idea as the
two-tangler self-host check (§13).

The second reader runs the grammar of §sec:read-grammar as code. The `peg` crate
turns the grammar into a parser at compile time. `peg` is a test-only dependency,
so it never links into the shipped binary. The grammar tokenizes one line into a
list of `Seg` atoms — text, a chunk use, a quote open, or a quote close. The
lowering then turns the atoms into events with the state rules of §sec:read-rules:
the chunk counter, the open chunk, the `%def` wait, and the cross-line quote. So
the grammar owns the shape and the lowering owns the state, the same split the hand
reader makes, but the tokenizer is a declarative grammar, not a loop.

The grammar resolves each escape (`@@<<` to the literal `@<<`) inside the text run, so
an escaped bracket never becomes a use or a quote. It marks a text run at each use,
each quote open, and each quote close. The lowering drops an empty run before a
marker but always keeps the last run of a line, so the empty-text rule of
§sec:read-rules holds. The `docs_out` rule scans a line that starts outside a quote;
`docs_in` scans a line that starts inside one; each returns the atoms and whether the
quote is still open, so the lowering carries the open state to the next line.
